 33: 
 34: The product problem is not primarily missing infrastructure. It is that users cannot tell:
 35: 1. what the AI will read,
 36: 2. whether it will use the web,
 37: 3. what it will create or alter,
 38: 4. where outputs will land,
 39: 5. whether a result was actually based on selected notes, web evidence, or a model-only answer,
 40: 6. what to do after adding a note,
 41: 7. how their current cards relate to a larger goal/capstone.
 42: 
 43: Your job is to make the existing system legible, trustworthy, touch-first, and goal-directed.
 44: 
 45: ============================================================
 46: PRODUCT NORTH STAR
 47: ============================================================
 48: 
 49: The app’s core promise is:
 50: 
 51: “Turn a goal into an evidence-backed capstone by capturing, organizing, investigating, practicing, and building from small units of knowledge.”
 52: 
 53: The app should feel like:
 54: - Unix composability for mobile
 55: - a focused Star Trek / AVP-inspired mission console
 56: - not a noisy sci-fi skin
 57: - Vim-like in conceptual simplicity and keyboard/command acceleration
 58: - touch-first, discoverable, and accessible for normal mobile use
 59: 
 60: The design must use the HUD aesthetic to communicate state, provenance, context, and progress. Do not use it as decorative visual clutter.
 61: 
 62: The app must preserve the powerful pipeline model:
 63: - Cards are the universal stream.
 64: - Selection is stdin.
 65: - New results are stdout.
 66: - Commands remain composable.
 67: - The agent is a visible, inspectable command stage—not an invisible magical force.
 68: 
 69: ============================================================
 70: NON-NEGOTIABLE UX RULES
 71: ============================================================
 72: 
 73: 1. Every AI operation must have explicit scope.
 74: Before the user runs it, the UI must state:
 75: - what context will be read,
 76: - whether web search is enabled,
 77: - what kind of output will be created,
 78: - where the output will be created.
 79: 
 80: 2. Every AI operation must have a run receipt.
 81: After it completes, show:
 82: - operation name
 83: - number/types of selected input cards
 84: - whether web search was used
 85: - number of search results and sources actually retained/opened, when applicable
 86: - number/types of cards created
 87: - destination group/workspace
 88: - provenance/citations where available
 89: - direct actions: View output, Undo, Inspect run
 90: 
 91: 3. Never imply that “Ask” browsed the web if it did not.
 92: The current `search` command is separate from `ask`; preserve that truth in the UI and implementation.
 93: 
 94: 4. Make web research a deliberate first-class workflow.
 95: It must be visibly distinct from “explain selected notes.”
 96: 
 97: 5. Capture must lead somewhere.
 98: After adding a note, source, question, or experiment, present obvious next actions rather than silently returning the user to the deck.
 99: 
100: 6. Use touch-friendly controls:
101: - minimum roughly 44x44 point targets
102: - text labels on important actions
103: - visible selection count
104: - one dominant primary action per state
105: - no reliance on swipe gestures or icons alone
106: - readable contrast in dark and light themes
107: - respect reduced-motion settings
108: 
109: 7. Keep command-line power users happy without requiring terminal literacy.
110: All important commands must be reachable with labeled touch actions and also expressible through the command palette.
111: 
112: ============================================================
113: IMPLEMENTATION PLAN
114: ============================================================
115: 
116: Work in phases. At the end of each phase:
117: - run typecheck/lint/tests using the repo’s actual scripts,
118: - fix failures,
119: - summarize modified files and behavior,
120: - do not proceed to the next phase until the current phase is coherent.
121: 
122: Prefer small, reviewable commits if git is available.
123: 
124: ============================================================
125: PHASE 0 — RECONNAISSANCE AND IMPLEMENTATION PLAN
126: ============================================================
127: 
128: First:
129: 1. Inspect the project tree and identify:
130:    - root app/screen entry points
131:    - controller/presenter state and actions
132:    - card entity and card type registry
133:    - pipeline runner, command interfaces, and operation-result handling
134:    - agent gateway behavior, including no-key/failure behavior
135:    - search gateway and extraction gateway behavior
136:    - current tests and test commands
137: 2. Identify exact files/components responsible for:
138:    - canvas/deck screen
139:    - selection UI
140:    - command palette
141:    - note/source capture
142:    - pipeline execution
143:    - operation result feedback
144:    - settings and assistant profiles
145: 3. Produce a concise implementation plan in `docs/goal-workspace-implementation-plan.md`.
146: 
147: The plan must map each requested user-facing feature to existing architecture and files. It must explicitly call out:
148: - current ask behavior vs current search behavior
149: - where false-positive “AI worked” behavior may occur during gateway failures/fallbacks
150: - how undo can be implemented safely with existing repositories
151: - a backwards-compatible migration strategy for persisted cards/workspaces/settings
152: 
153: Do not make broad code changes before this document exists.
154: 
155: ============================================================
156: PHASE 1 — DOMAIN MODEL FOR GOALS, PROJECTS, AND PROVENANCE
157: ============================================================
158: 
159: Extend the data model carefully and backwards compatibly.
160: 
161: A. Add a workspace-level mission/goal model.
162: 
163: A workspace should optionally support:
164: - goalTitle
165: - goalDescription
166: - successCriteria: string[]
167: - targetDeliverable
168: - currentPhase or status
169: - createdAt / updatedAt as appropriate
170: 
171: Do not break existing persisted workspaces. Missing mission fields must gracefully resolve to empty/default state.
172: 
173: B. Add user-facing semantic knowledge roles.
174: 
175: Do not necessarily replace internal `Card.type` values immediately. Instead add a compatible semantic role/category field or mapping suitable for:
176: - goal
177: - question
178: - concept
179: - source
180: - experiment
181: - claim
182: - task
183: - deliverable
184: 
185: Requirements:
186: - Existing cards must remain readable.
187: - Existing card type registry and learning behavior must continue to work.
188: - A semantic role should be optional, editable, and displayed in the UI.
189: - Keep study mechanics separate: a semantic role does not automatically make a card reviewable.
190: 
191: C. Add structured provenance.
192: 
193: Create domain types sufficient to explain how a card was created. At minimum capture:
194: - creation mode: manual | agent | search | extraction | command
195: - operation/run ID
196: - source card IDs read
197: - search query, if used
198: - source URLs used or retained
199: - active assistant profile/model if available
200: - timestamps
201: - citation/excerpt links when available
202: 
203: Keep provenance compact and persistable in AsyncStorage. Existing cards without provenance must work.
204: 
205: D. Add an operation/run receipt entity.
206: 
207: Create a persisted or safely reconstructable operation log entry, with:
208: - id
209: - command/action name
210: - workspaceId
211: - parentId/destination
212: - inputCardIds
213: - createdCardIds
214: - updatedCardIds if applicable
215: - deletedCard snapshots or IDs if needed for undo
216: - web/search metadata
217: - model/profile metadata
218: - startedAt/completedAt
219: - concise human-readable summary
220: 
221: Use a repository interface plus AsyncStorage implementation if persistence is practical. If not, maintain a robust session-level history and explain why in the documentation. Prefer persistence.
222: 
223: E. Tests.
224: 
225: Add entity/use-case tests for:
226: - backwards-compatible workspace/card defaults
227: - provenance creation
228: - run receipt creation
229: - serialization/deserialization
230: - semantic role behavior
231: - no accidental scheduling behavior changes
232: 
233: ============================================================
234: PHASE 2 — EXPLICIT AI ACTIONS AND PREFLIGHT
235: ============================================================
236: 
237: Replace ambiguous generic entry points with explicit user-facing actions while preserving underlying pipeline commands.
238: 
239: Add a unified `AgentRunRequest` / `AgentScope` concept in the domain/use-case layer.
240: 
241: Scope must distinguish:
242: - `selected-only`: read exactly selected cards
243: - `workspace`: read selected cards plus a bounded relevant workspace context
244: - `web`: conduct web research from a query
245: - `selected-plus-web`: selected cards plus research
246: 
247: Do not silently send the entire workspace to a model. For workspace scope, implement a deterministic bounded strategy and expose it in the receipt:
248: - selected cards first
249: - active parent/group breadcrumb/context
250: - recent or explicitly linked related cards
251: - strict max-card and max-character budget
252: - clearly report truncation
253: 
254: Create clear operation presets/actions:
255: 
256: 1. Explain selected notes
257: - Inputs: selected note/source/chunk/concept cards
258: - Web: off by default
259: - Output: one or more grounded explanation/concept cards
260: - Clearly label that only selected content is used unless the user changes scope
261: 
262: 2. Research on the web
263: - Inputs: optional selected cards plus user query
264: - Web: on
265: - Output: source assessment cards and optionally extracted/cited source cards
266: - Never claim a source was opened/extracted if it was not
267: 
268: 3. Find prerequisites
269: - Inputs: selected cards and/or workspace mission
270: - Output: prerequisite concept/question cards and/or a gap assessment
271: - If no model is available, fail clearly rather than fabricating a “successful” result
272: 
273: 4. Plan experiment
274: - Inputs: selected notes/questions plus optional goal
275: - Output: experiment card with hypothesis, method, required materials/tools, measurement, expected result, and next action
276: 
277: 5. Make study cards
278: - Inputs: selected learning content
279: - Output: recall/cloze cards and optionally enroll in FSRS only after explicit confirmation or a clearly named pipeline step
280: 
281: 6. Plan capstone
282: - Inputs: workspace mission, selected cards, and optionally web evidence
283: - Output: milestone/task/deliverable cards or a structured plan group
284: 
285: Keep existing terminal commands valid. Add aliases/macros only where helpful. Do not remove `ask`, but reframe it in the UI as “Ask / custom” and show scope explicitly.
286: 
287: Build an AI preflight bottom sheet/modal for each explicit action.
288: 
289: The preflight must show:
290: - action name and plain-English purpose
291: - selected item count and a short list/preview
292: - context scope selector
293: - web state
294: - output types
295: - destination
296: - model/profile when applicable
297: - warning if no API key/model is configured
298: - estimated behavior, not invented token/cost data unless actual provider metadata exists
299: - Run button with exact language, e.g. “Research 3 selected notes on the web”
300: 
301: Ensure every AI action has a deterministic testable request object before it reaches a gateway.
302: 
303: ============================================================
304: PHASE 3 — REAL WEB RESEARCH WORKFLOW
305: ============================================================
306: 
307: Implement a composable research flow using existing search and extraction architecture.
308: 
309: Do not make the LLM claim it searched. The orchestration layer must perform actual search via `SearchGateway`.
310: 
311: A. Search result normalization.
312: 
313: Define normalized research result/source types including:
314: - title
315: - canonical URL
316: - snippet
317: - domain
318: - query
319: - access timestamp
320: - rank
321: - source kind / evidence type when determinable
322: - relevance assessment
323: - credibility signals
324: - limitations/cautions
325: - keep/reject state
326: - extracted text state
327: - citation metadata
328: 
329: B. Research pipeline.
330: 
331: The explicit “Research on the web” action should:
332: 1. obtain/refine the query
333: 2. execute actual search through SearchGateway
334: 3. save a search/run artifact with provenance
335: 4. display search results as inspectable source candidates
336: 5. allow user to keep, reject, open, and extract source candidates
337: 6. optionally use the model to synthesize a cited research brief only from selected/retained results and extracted text
338: 7. create source/claim/concept cards with citations and provenance
339: 8. generate a run receipt
340: 
341: If automatic source scoring is implemented, it must be transparent and advisory. Use simple explainable heuristics where possible:
342: - first-party docs / official domains
343: - academic or peer-reviewed signals
344: - author/date availability
345: - domain repetition/deduplication
346: - alignment with query
347: - limitation/caution labels
348: 
349: Do not present opaque credibility scores as factual truth. Use labels such as “High relevance,” “Official documentation,” “Tutorial; verify against specification,” or “Limited provenance.”
350: 
351: C. UI.
352: 
353: Create an inspectable research results screen/sheet integrated with the existing canvas model:
354: - source candidate cards
355: - relevance/evidence-role/caution metadata
356: - large actions: Keep, Extract, Reject, Open
357: - “Create cited brief” only enabled when retained evidence exists
358: - full provenance visible on source/derived cards
359: 
360: D. Error behavior.
361: 
362: Handle:
363: - no network
364: - no results
365: - malformed DuckDuckGo results
366: - blocked extraction
367: - missing API key
368: - model errors
369: - partial success
370: 
371: Partial success must still create truthful receipts. Never silently replace a failed external model call with generic local content unless the UI clearly labels it as a local fallback and the user opted into that behavior.
372: 
373: ============================================================
374: PHASE 4 — MISSION HOME AND GAP REPORT
375: ============================================================
376: 
377: Shift the default workspace experience from a generic deck to a mission-oriented workbench while preserving the one-canvas philosophy.
378: 
379: Add a compact top-of-canvas “Mission Control” module that appears when a workspace has a goal:
380: - mission title
381: - success criterion / target deliverable
382: - counts for blockers, prerequisites, active experiments, and next tasks
383: - one high-confidence next action
384: - concise status language
385: 
386: Do not build a complicated dashboard. This should be a compact module that feeds the same card canvas.
387: 
388: Implement a “Status / Gap Report” action that answers:
389: 
390: “What do I have, what do I want, what is missing, and what should I do next?”
391: 
392: The report must include:
393: - Mission/desired outcome
394: - Current evidence: sources, concepts, claims, experiments, tasks, deliverables
395: - Known blockers/questions
396: - Missing prerequisites
397: - Evidence gaps
398: - Recommended next actions
399: - Completion/maturity indicators that are explicitly heuristic, not falsely precise
400: 
401: Implement this first as:
402: - a structured report card/group generated deterministically from the card graph where possible
403: - optional agent enrichment with clearly declared scope
404: - provenance showing whether each portion was deterministic, model-generated, or web-grounded
405: 
406: If the workspace has no mission:
407: - show an elegant empty-state prompt to define one
408: - allow users to continue using the workspace as before
409: 
410: ============================================================
411: PHASE 5 — CAPTURE AND SELECTION FLOWS
412: ============================================================
413: 
414: Improve capture and selection feedback.
415: 
416: A. Capture flow.
417: 
418: After a user creates a note/source/question/experiment:
419: - show a compact success confirmation
420: - state exactly where it was added
421: - keep context/selection if appropriate
422: - present 2–3 context-sensitive next actions
423: 
424: Examples:
425: After adding a note:
426: - Attach to mission
427: - Find prerequisites
428: - Ask about this note
429: 
430: After adding a source:
431: - Extract key ideas
432: - Assess source quality
433: - Turn into study cards
434: 
435: After adding an experiment:
436: - Add measurement
437: - Identify required prerequisites
438: - Create next task
439: 
440: B. Selection tray.
441: 
442: When items are selected, use a visible bottom action tray with:
443: - selection count
444: - clear selection action
445: - Explain
446: - Research
447: - Connect
448: - Study
449: - More
450: 
451: These must open preflight, not execute hidden AI behavior.
452: 
453: Keep command palette access available. Buttons may populate/launch equivalent commands, but the touch workflow must expose scope before execution.
454: 
455: C. Output behavior.
456: 
457: For pipeline continuity:
458: - newly created result cards should be visibly highlighted
459: - optionally selected for the next pipeline step, but this must be communicated
460: - do not silently replace the user’s selection without a visible result state
461: - show the parent/destination clearly
462: 
463: ============================================================
464: PHASE 6 — COMMAND PALETTE EVOLUTION
465: ============================================================
466: 
467: Preserve the Unix terminal philosophy but make commands human-readable.
468: 
469: Update command presentation so each command has:
470: - terminal name
471: - plain-language label
472: - one-line purpose
473: - input requirement
474: - output behavior
475: - web indicator if relevant
476: 
477: Canonical user-facing actions:
478: - Capture
479: - Explain
480: - Research web
481: - Find prerequisites
482: - Connect
483: - Plan experiment
484: - Make study cards
485: - Review
486: - Status
487: - Plan capstone
488: 
489: Suggested command aliases/macros:
490: - `/research`
491: - `/prereqs`
492: - `/experiment`
493: - `/study`
494: - `/status`
495: - `/build`
496: 
497: The command palette should:
498: - retain custom command definitions and pipelines
499: - support typed pipelines
500: - show selected input context
501: - differentiate commands that require a selection from commands that start fresh
502: - reveal actual side effects before execution where possible
503: - preserve current pinned-command behavior
504: 
505: Do not make every action a separate screen. Use the canvas, bottom sheets, and the existing modal model appropriately.
506: 
507: ============================================================
508: PHASE 7 — VISUAL SYSTEM
509: ============================================================
510: 
511: Evolve the existing terminal/HUD theme rather than replacing it.
512: 
513: Design requirements:
514: - Current dark/light theme support must continue.
515: - Keep the mono typography selectively for commands, metadata, telemetry, IDs, and structured system labels.
516: - Use a readable system sans for body content if already available or safely add one only if appropriate.
517: - Accent colors should communicate semantic categories or state, not decoration.
518: - Preserve reduced-motion behavior.
519: - Avoid excessive borders, tiny text, low contrast, overly dense panels, or ambiguous icon-only controls.
520: - Keep large touch targets and clear labels.
521: 
522: Use HUD language sparingly and meaningfully:
523: - MISSION
524: - CONTEXT
525: - WEB EVIDENCE
526: - OUTPUT
527: - RUN COMPLETE
528: - BLOCKER
529: - NEXT ACTION
530: - PROVENANCE
531: 
532: No fake telemetry, random percentages, decorative crosshairs, or animations that imply capability.
533: 
534: ============================================================
535: PHASE 8 — UNDO, ERROR STATES, AND TRUST
536: ============================================================
537: 
538: Implement reliable undo for the immediately completed operation.
539: 
540: Minimum:
541: - Undo creates: remove cards created by the operation if they have not been materially edited since creation
542: - Undo grouping: restore children’s prior parent IDs and remove created group when safe
543: - Undo move: restore previous workspace/parent when snapshots exist
544: - Do not claim undo succeeded if it only partially succeeded
545: - If undo cannot safely apply, explain why
546: 
547: For destructive actions, preserve or add confirmation where appropriate.
548: 
549: Add visible and actionable error UI for:
550: - missing API key
551: - no selected cards when action requires selection
552: - failed search
553: - failed extraction
554: - failed model call
555: - unsupported pipeline state
556: - no results
557: - offline/network errors
558: 
559: Use errors that tell the user what to do next.
560: 
561: ============================================================
562: PHASE 9 — TESTS, DOCUMENTATION, AND QUALITY GATES
563: ============================================================
564: 
565: Add and update tests at the appropriate layers.
566: 
567: Must test:
568: - existing pipeline behavior remains intact
569: - old workspace/card storage remains readable
570: - selection scope is correctly passed to agent/search flows
571: - `ask` without web does not invoke SearchGateway
572: - research action does invoke SearchGateway
573: - agent synthesis only receives retained/selected evidence as designed
574: - run receipts accurately record inputs, outputs, and web usage
575: - undo behavior for created cards and group/move where implemented
576: - missing key and gateway failures produce explicit failure states
577: - semantic roles do not accidentally affect FSRS scheduling
578: - gap report deterministic portions work without an API key
579: 
580: Update:
581: - README.md: revised product description and major user flows
582: - terminal-learn-prd.md: add an implementation-status section or revise carefully without deleting useful original product principles
583: - docs/goal-workspace-implementation-plan.md: final implementation notes, migration notes, known limitations
584: - any relevant architecture documentation
585: 
586: Use concise comments only where necessary. Do not add noisy logging, API keys, secrets, hardcoded remote endpoints beyond existing gateway patterns, or unbounded context uploads.
587: 
588: ============================================================
589: ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
590: ============================================================
591: 
592: A user can:
593: 1. Create/select a workspace mission and success criteria.
594: 2. Add a note and immediately see meaningful next actions.
595: 3. Select multiple notes and tap Explain; before execution they can see exactly what will be read and what will be created.
596: 4. Select notes and tap Research web; before execution they can see web is enabled.
597: 5. Inspect actual web results, keep/reject/extract candidates, and see warnings/quality labels.
598: 6. Create a cited research brief only from retained evidence.
599: 7. Inspect any generated card’s provenance.
600: 8. See a run receipt after every AI/web operation.
601: 9. Undo the last operation when safe.
602: 10. View a useful status/gap report for a mission.
603: 11. Still run original commands and typed pipelines.
604: 12. Still use chunking, recall, spaced repetition, review, grouping, custom commands, assistant profiles, and local persistence.
605: 13. Understand from the UI that “Ask” and “Research web” are different operations.
606: 14. Use the app comfortably one-handed on a phone without needing terminal knowledge.
607: 
608: ============================================================
609: EXECUTION RULES
610: ============================================================
611: 
612: - Work directly in the repository.
613: - Do not ask me broad product questions; make reasonable, documented decisions based on this specification and existing code.
614: - If a requirement conflicts with the current architecture, choose the smallest clean change and document the tradeoff.
615: - Maintain TypeScript strictness and existing coding conventions.
616: - Reuse existing entities/use cases/gateways where appropriate.
617: - Prefer adapter interfaces over framework imports in use cases.
618: - Do not call network APIs from UI components.
619: - Do not hide failures with fake success states.
620: - Do not remove working existing capabilities merely to simplify the implementation.
621: - Avoid massive rewrites. Refactor incrementally.
622: - Test after each phase and fix regressions before proceeding.
623: - At the end, provide:
624:   1. a concise feature summary,
625:   2. architecture changes,
626:   3. list of changed files,
627:   4. test commands run and results,
628:   5. migration/storage notes,
629:   6. remaining limitations and recommended next steps.
630: 
631: Start with Phase 0 now.
